      "dom_id": "question-36098",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{2(x-2)^{8}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{2(x-2)^{8}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $2$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273867/assets/81491bb2e7201fd249d6c3ac3a7ec1d902f612887cb59a5155b60308f42e2f8f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({2}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({2}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$",
